.TH SYSUP 1 "2026-11-04" "sysup 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sysup \- update and tidy up an entire Arch Linux system with one command
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sysup
[\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sysup
updates everything on an Arch Linux system and then cleans up after itself.
It finds what is installed and runs only what applies:
.IP \(bu 2
shows Arch news posted since your last upgrade, so you see manual interventions first
.IP \(bu 2
refreshes old mirrorlists (reflector or rate-mirrors), updates archlinux-keyring first
.IP \(bu 2
runs a full repo + AUR upgrade with paru, yay, pikaur or trizen (or plain pacman)
.IP \(bu 2
updates Flatpak, Snap, firmware (fwupd), developer toolchains (rustup, cargo, pipx,
uv, npm, bun, deno, gh extensions, tldr), shell and editor plugins (oh\-my\-zsh,
fisher, tpm, lazy.nvim) and dotfile frameworks (Caelestia)
.IP \(bu 2
removes orphans, trims the pacman and AUR build caches, vacuums the journal and
deletes stale thumbnails; with \fB\-\-deep\fR also language caches, dangling
container images, old coredumps and old trash
.IP \(bu 2
reports .pacnew files, failed services, packages needing a rebuild and whether a
reboot is needed
.PP
Run it as your normal user. It asks for your sudo password once and keeps it
alive for the whole run.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Never ask questions; pass \-\-noconfirm to everything.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Show every command that would run without changing anything.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-check
List available repo, AUR and Flatpak updates, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-deep
Also do a deep clean.
.TP
.B \-\-devel
Also rebuild \-git/VCS AUR packages.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-clean
Skip the cleanup step.
.TP
.BI \-\-only " STEPS"
Run only these steps (comma separated).
.TP
.BI \-\-skip " STEPS"
Skip these steps (comma separated).
.TP
.BI \-\-helper " NAME"
Use this AUR helper: paru, yay, pikaur, trizen or pacman.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list\-steps
List all steps with a description.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
Read an extra config file after the default ones.
.TP
.B \-\-edit\-config
Open your per\-user config in \fB$VISUAL\fR/\fB$EDITOR\fR, creating it if needed.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable colours (the \fBNO_COLOR\fR environment variable works too).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH STEPS
news, mirrors, keyring, packages, flatpak, snap, firmware, devtools, plugins,
dotfiles, extra, clean, checks
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/sysup.conf
System\-wide configuration. Every option is documented in it.
.TP
.I ~/.config/sysup/sysup.conf
Per\-user configuration, read after the system file.
.TP
.I ~/.config/sysup/hooks.d/
Executable scripts here are run during the \fBextra\fR step.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/sysup/history.log
One line per run with the result of each step.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 if any step failed, 130 when interrupted.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B sysup
Update and clean everything, asking before risky steps.
.TP
.B sysup \-yd
Unattended run with deep clean.
.TP
.B sysup \-\-only clean \-d
Only free up disk space.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pacman (8),
.BR paru (8),
.BR yay (8),
.BR paccache (8),
.BR pacdiff (8),
.BR flatpak (1),
.BR fwupdmgr (1)
